        {
          "@type": "ListItem",
          position: 1,
          name: "Home",
          item: "https://www.allmetalbrake.com/",
        },
        {
          "@type": "ListItem",
          position: 2,
          name: "Best Lists",
          item: "https://www.allmetalbrake.com/best-of/",
        },
        {
          "@type": "ListItem",
          position: 3,
          name: "Ceiling Paint",
          item: "https://www.allmetalbrake.com/best-ceiling-paint/",
        },
      ],
    },
    {
      "@type": "FAQPage",
      mainEntity: [
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Is ceiling paint really different from wall paint?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Yes. Ceiling paint is usually thicker to reduce drips and spatter, flatter to hide imperfections in raking light, and sometimes has a longer open time to help keep a wet edge. Wall paint is designed to be more washable and comes in more sheens. Using the right product for each surface makes the job easier and the result better."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Does a ceiling need two coats?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Most ceilings benefit from two coats, especially when changing color, painting new drywall or covering patches. A premium high-hide ceiling paint over a similar white in good condition may cover in one coat. Two thin coats generally give a more uniform finish than one thick coat and help hide roller marks and flashing."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "What causes roller marks on a ceiling, and how do I avoid them?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Roller marks usually come from letting the wet edge dry, pressing too hard, using too little paint or using a low-quality roller cover. Keep a wet edge, work in small sections, load the roller evenly, use a quality cover with the right nap, and finish with light parallel strokes. Ultra-flat ceiling paint also helps hide marks."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "When does a ceiling need primer?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Prime new drywall with a PVA primer, seal stains with a stain-blocking primer, and spot-prime patches. Previously painted ceilings in good condition usually need only cleaning before painting. If changing from a dark color to white or covering smoke damage, a primer improves coverage and prevents bleed-through."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Will ceiling paint work on walls too?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "You can, but it is not ideal for most rooms. Flat ceiling paint is not designed to be scrubbed and will mark easily from hands, furniture and cleaning. In low-traffic areas like closets or guest rooms it can work, but wall paint in eggshell or matte is more durable for walls."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Which paint is best for a bathroom ceiling?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "A mildew-resistant paint such as Zinsser Perma-White in a satin or eggshell finish is a strong choice for bathroom ceilings, because it resists mildew growth and can be wiped clean. Make sure the exhaust fan works and is vented outside, and clean any existing mildew thoroughly before painting. Paint alone will not fix a ventilation problem."
          }
        },
