    </div>

    <!-- Content -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pb-16 lg:pb-24">
      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
        <p class="text-2xl font-medium text-gray-900 mb-8 italic">
          Nobody admires a ceiling until something is wrong with it: a shiny stripe where the roller overlapped, a ghost of an old water stain, a fleck of white on the hardwood below. Ceiling paint exists to prevent exactly those things. It is flatter than wall paint so light does not reveal every seam, thicker so it does not rain down on you, and often whiter so the room feels brighter. Pick the right one for the room and roll it the right way, and the ceiling disappears, which is the whole point.
        </p>

        <QuickPicks products={PRODUCTS} />


        <!-- Picks -->
        <div id="top-picks" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. The 7 Best Ceiling Paints, Ranked
          </h2>

          <p>
            We ranked these ceiling paints and primers from manufacturer data sheets and labels, covering sheen, spatter and dry-time claims, coverage, stain-blocking and mildew-resistance features, along with warranty terms and consistent patterns in owner feedback. We did not run our own lab or room tests. Where a point rests on a label or on owner reports, we say so.
          </p>

          {PRODUCTS.map((product, index) => (
            <ProductCard product={product} index={index} />
          ))}
        </div>

        <div id="why-ceiling-paint" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. What Ceiling Paint Does That Wall Paint Does Not
          </h2>

          <p>
            Ceiling paint is not just white wall paint in a different can. Manufacturers adjust three things for overhead use: thickness, sheen and spatter. A thicker, more viscous paint stays on the roller instead of dripping, and is less likely to spray off the roller in fine droplets as it spins. That alone makes painting overhead much less messy and safer for the floors and furniture below.
          </p>

          <p>
            Sheen is the second difference. Ceiling paints are almost always flat or ultra-flat, because ceilings are lit at a low angle by windows and lamps, which exaggerates any shine. Even a small amount of sheen will show roller laps, patches and seams in that raking light. Flat paint scatters light evenly and hides those imperfections.
          </p>

          <p>
            Ceiling paints also often have a longer open time, the window during which wet paint can blend with the next section without leaving a visible lap. Overhead work is slower and more awkward than walls, so that extra time makes it easier to keep a wet edge across a large ceiling.
          </p>

          <p>
            Can you use wall paint on a ceiling? Yes, in a flat finish, but you give up spatter control and some hiding ability. Can you use ceiling paint on walls? It works in low-traffic rooms, but flat ceiling paint is not designed to be scrubbed and will mark easily. Each product is designed for its job, and ceiling paint costs little more than wall paint.
          </p>

          <p>
            A quick way to judge a ceiling paint in the store is the label language. Look for terms like spatter-resistant, ultra-flat or dead-flat, and extended open time, plus a coverage figure and recoat time on the data sheet. A can that simply says interior flat white, with no ceiling-specific claims, is usually a general-purpose flat paint that will work but give you less help overhead.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="sheen-light" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            3. Sheen and Light: Why Flat Wins Overhead
          </h2>

          <p>
            Light is the reason ceilings look bad. Daylight from a window, or a lamp near a wall, hits the ceiling at a shallow angle, and at that angle every bump, roller ridge and difference in sheen casts a shadow or reflects differently. A patch that is invisible when you look straight up can show clearly from across the room in the late afternoon.
          </p>

          <p>
            Flat and ultra-flat paints minimize that effect. Ultra-flat ceiling paints have very little sheen, so the whole ceiling reflects light the same way even where the paint is slightly thicker or a patch absorbed it differently. That is why premium ceiling paints are worth their price in rooms with large windows, skylights or wall-mounted lighting.
          </p>

          <p>
            There are exceptions. Bathrooms, kitchens and laundry rooms see steam, grease and moisture, and a flat finish can stain or grow mildew. In those rooms, a mildew-resistant paint in a matte, eggshell or satin finish is often a better choice, accepting slightly more visible imperfections in exchange for a surface that can be wiped clean.
          </p>

