        <div id="color" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. Bright White, Tinted White or Color
          </h2>

          <p>
            Most ceilings are painted white because white reflects the most light back into the room, making spaces feel taller and brighter. Ceiling whites are usually formulated with a high light reflectance value, a measure of how much light a color reflects. Untinted ceiling white sits near the top of that scale.
          </p>

          <p>
            Pure bright white can look stark or bluish next to warm wall colors. Many designers tint the ceiling slightly, either a white with a touch of warmth or a very diluted version of the wall color. Ask the paint store to tint ceiling paint at a quarter or half strength of the wall color for a ceiling that relates to the walls without darkening the room.
          </p>

          <p>
            Colored ceilings are a deliberate design choice. Darker ceilings make rooms feel lower and cozier, which can work in a large bedroom, dining room or den with tall ceilings. If you go darker, choose a paint designed for the color, since tinting a white ceiling base heavily can cause poor coverage. Premium wall paints in flat or matte finishes are often used for colored ceilings.
          </p>

          <p>
            Whatever color you choose, test it. Paint a sample board and hold it overhead in the room in morning and evening light. Ceilings are seen in shadow most of the time, so colors look darker overhead than on a chip in the store. A shade that looks right on the wall may look a step darker on the ceiling.
          </p>

          <p>
            Remember that ceilings borrow color from everything around them. Strong wall colors, wood floors and even green lawns outside a window bounce tinted light upward, so a white ceiling in a room with deep blue walls will read faintly blue. That is another reason to look at samples in place rather than judging white chips under store lighting.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="stains-prep" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. Stains, Cracks and Texture: Prep Before Paint
          </h2>

          <p>
            Ceiling paint alone will not hide water stains, smoke damage, nicotine or marker. Those stains bleed through water-based paint, sometimes reappearing within hours. Seal them first with a stain-blocking primer, such as a shellac-based or oil-based primer, then apply ceiling paint. Before sealing a water stain, make sure the leak is fixed and the drywall is dry and sound.
          </p>

          <p>
            Fill cracks and nail pops before painting. Hairline cracks can be filled with lightweight spackle; recurring cracks at drywall joints may need retaping. Nail pops should be reset or replaced with drywall screws and covered with compound. Sand patches smooth and spot-prime them so they do not flash through the finish.
          </p>

          <p>
            Clean the ceiling before painting, especially in kitchens and bathrooms. Grease and soap residue prevent adhesion. Wipe with a mild degreasing cleaner, rinse with clean water and let it dry. In bathrooms, treat any mildew with an appropriate cleaner first, rinse and dry thoroughly, and make sure the exhaust fan is working before you paint.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">SAFETY FIRST</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">Test textured ceilings in older homes before you scrape or sand.</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">Popcorn and acoustic ceiling textures applied before the 1980s may contain asbestos, and paint in homes built before 1978 may contain lead. Have a sample tested before scraping, sanding or removing texture. Painting over intact texture is usually safer than disturbing it.</p>
          </div>

          <p>
            Textured ceilings need special care. Popcorn ceilings absorb paint and can loosen when wet, so use a thick-nap roller and light pressure, and consider spraying with a <a href="/best-paint-sprayer/">paint sprayer</a> to avoid pulling texture off. Knockdown and orange-peel textures roll more easily but still need a thicker roller nap than smooth ceilings.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="tools" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. Tools and Setup for Painting Overhead
          </h2>

          <p>
            Use a sturdy roller frame and an extension pole so you can paint from the floor, keeping your arms lower, which reduces fatigue and improves control. A pole that extends from about 2 to 4 feet works well for standard 8 to 9 foot ceilings; taller ceilings need longer poles. Make sure the frame screws firmly onto the pole and does not wobble.
          </p>

          <p>
            Choose the right roller cover. For smooth or lightly textured ceilings, a 3/8 to 1/2 inch nap is typical; for heavier textures, 3/4 inch or more. Woven microfiber and high-quality synthetic covers hold more paint and release it evenly, reducing spatter and lap marks. Cheap covers shed fibers and spatter, which shows clearly on a white ceiling.
          </p>

          <p>
            For cutting in, use a quality 2 to 2.5 inch angled sash brush. A small stepladder, rated for your weight plus tools and paint, is safer than a chair. Set it up on a level surface and do not stand on the top step. Keep a damp rag nearby to wipe drips immediately.
          </p>

          <p>
            Protect the room. Move furniture to the center or out of the room, cover floors with canvas drop cloths that will not slip, and cover furniture with plastic. Remove or tape over light fixtures, smoke detector covers and ceiling fan canopies. Wear safety glasses or goggles and a cap, as even low-spatter paint will occasionally drip.
          </p>

