        <div id="technique" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. Rolling Technique for a Seamless Finish
          </h2>

          <p>
            Cut in first. Paint a 2 to 3 inch band along the edges where the ceiling meets the walls and around fixtures. To avoid lap marks between the brushed band and the rolled field, cut in only one wall at a time and roll that area while the band is still wet, rather than cutting in the whole room first.
          </p>

          <p>
            Load the roller evenly on a tray or bucket grid, then roll in sections about 4 by 4 feet. Start a few inches from the previous wet edge and roll back into it, spreading the paint in a W or zigzag pattern and then filling in with parallel strokes. Finish each section with light, straight passes in the same direction for a uniform texture.
          </p>

          <p>
            Keep a wet edge across the whole ceiling. Work across the shorter dimension of the room so each new section overlaps paint that is still wet. Do not stop in the middle of a ceiling for a break; finish the entire ceiling in one session. If you must stop, end at a natural break such as a beam or a corner.
          </p>

          <p>
            Many painters finish with the final strokes parallel to the main window light, which makes any remaining roller texture less visible. Two thin coats are better than one heavy coat. Wait the label recoat time, typically a few hours, then apply the second coat perpendicular to the first for more even coverage. Avoid going back over areas that have started to dry, which causes flashing and lap marks.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="room-guide" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            8. Picking Paint by Room and Ceiling Type
          </h2>

          <p>
            <strong>Living rooms and bright spaces.</strong> Choose an ultra-flat premium paint such as Benjamin Moore Waterborne Ceiling Paint or Sherwin-Williams Eminence. These rooms have the most raking light, which reveals every flaw in a lesser paint. <strong>Bedrooms and hallways.</strong> A good flat paint like BEHR Premium Plus is usually enough, especially on ceilings in good condition.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Bathrooms and laundry rooms.</strong> Use a mildew-resistant paint like Zinsser Perma-White in a satin or eggshell finish, and make sure the exhaust fan is working and vented outside. <strong>Kitchens.</strong> Grease and steam favor a matte or eggshell finish that can be wiped clean; clean thoroughly before painting.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>New drywall.</strong> Prime with a PVA drywall primer, then apply two coats of ceiling paint. <strong>Stained ceilings.</strong> Seal stains with a shellac or oil-based stain-blocking primer before painting. <strong>Popcorn or textured ceilings.</strong> Test for asbestos in older homes, then use a thick-nap roller or spray, with light pressure.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Porch ceilings and exterior soffits.</strong> Use exterior paint rather than interior ceiling paint, since these areas see humidity and temperature swings. Our guide to the <a href="/best-porch-paint/">best porch paint</a> covers porch ceilings, including the traditional haint blue. <strong>Basements and garages.</strong> Budget ceiling paints work well in utility spaces, and the same rooms often benefit from fresh floor paint; see our pick of the <a href="/best-concrete-floor-paint/">best concrete floor paint</a> for that project.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="coverage-cost" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            9. How Much Ceiling Paint You Need and What It Costs
          </h2>

          <p>
            Most ceiling paints list coverage around 350 to 400 square feet per gallon on smooth, primed surfaces, less on textured ceilings and new drywall. To estimate, multiply the room's length by its width. A 12 by 14 foot bedroom ceiling is 168 square feet, so one gallon usually covers two coats. A 20 by 20 foot living room needs about two gallons for two coats.
          </p>

