        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            10. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Painting over a water stain without sealing it</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Water stains bleed through ordinary ceiling paint, often within hours. Fix the leak, let the area dry, then seal the stain with a shellac or oil-based stain-blocking primer before painting. Ceiling paint labeled as stain-resistant may handle minor marks, but it is not a substitute for primer on real water or smoke damage.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Choosing a paint with too much sheen</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Even a small amount of sheen shows roller marks, patches and seams in raking light. Most ceilings look best in flat or ultra-flat paint. Use a satin or eggshell only where you need cleanability, such as bathrooms and kitchens, and accept that it will show more imperfections than flat paint.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Cutting in the whole room before rolling</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                If the brushed band along the walls dries before you roll next to it, the brushed and rolled areas can look different, creating a picture-frame effect. Cut in one wall at a time and roll that area while the band is still wet. This keeps the finish uniform across the edges.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Stopping halfway across the ceiling</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Taking a break mid-ceiling lets the wet edge dry, leaving a visible lap line when you resume. Plan to finish each ceiling in one session, and if you must stop, end at a natural break such as a corner or beam. Keep a wet edge by working across the room's shorter dimension.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Skipping primer on new drywall</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                New drywall and joint compound absorb paint unevenly, causing seams and patches to flash through the topcoat. A PVA drywall primer seals the surface so ceiling paint goes on evenly. Skipping it often means needing extra coats of finish paint, and the seams may still show in raking light.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <PlanTheJob />

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            11. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is ceiling paint really different from wall paint?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Yes. Ceiling paint is usually thicker to reduce drips and spatter, flatter to hide imperfections in raking light, and sometimes has a longer open time to help keep a wet edge. Wall paint is designed to be more washable and comes in more sheens. Using the right product for each surface makes the job easier and the result better.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Does a ceiling need two coats?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Most ceilings benefit from two coats, especially when changing color, painting new drywall or covering patches. A premium high-hide ceiling paint over a similar white in good condition may cover in one coat. Two thin coats generally give a more uniform finish than one thick coat and help hide roller marks and flashing.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What causes roller marks on a ceiling, and how do I avoid them?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Roller marks usually come from letting the wet edge dry, pressing too hard, using too little paint or using a low-quality roller cover. Keep a wet edge, work in small sections, load the roller evenly, use a quality cover with the right nap, and finish with light parallel strokes. Ultra-flat ceiling paint also helps hide marks.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">When does a ceiling need primer?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Prime new drywall with a PVA primer, seal stains with a stain-blocking primer, and spot-prime patches. Previously painted ceilings in good condition usually need only cleaning before painting. If changing from a dark color to white or covering smoke damage, a primer improves coverage and prevents bleed-through.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Will ceiling paint work on walls too?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                You can, but it is not ideal for most rooms. Flat ceiling paint is not designed to be scrubbed and will mark easily from hands, furniture and cleaning. In low-traffic areas like closets or guest rooms it can work, but wall paint in eggshell or matte is more durable for walls.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Which paint is best for a bathroom ceiling?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                A mildew-resistant paint such as Zinsser Perma-White in a satin or eggshell finish is a strong choice for bathroom ceilings, because it resists mildew growth and can be wiped clean. Make sure the exhaust fan works and is vented outside, and clean any existing mildew thoroughly before painting. Paint alone will not fix a ventilation problem.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How do I paint a popcorn ceiling?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Test for asbestos first if the home was built before the 1980s. If the texture is safe and intact, use a thick-nap roller with light pressure or spray with a paint sprayer to avoid loosening the texture. Do not overwork the surface, and plan for more paint than a smooth ceiling, since texture absorbs more.
              </p>
            </div>

          </div>
